WEBVTT

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:16.790
Welcome to NASA Launchpad.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.340
I'm your host, Justin Tully.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.880
Alright, so when you hear the word NASA, what is the first thing you think of?

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:22.840
Space, right?

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:24.000
Yeah, of course.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:29.970
Because NASA is by far the premier space agency in the world, but NASA does a whole lot more

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.870
more than just work in space, you know? Like what? Well, let me give you a hint. What

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:39.700
do the letters of NASA stand for? It's the National Aeronautics and Space Administration,

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:45.200
right? The first A in NASA is Aeronautics. So that should tell you that NASA is heavily

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:50.880
involved in the study of aircraft. In fact, virtually every commercial and military aircraft

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:57.320
flown in the U.S. since 1915 has benefited from testing done at NASA. Of course, most

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.860
aircraft of the past and of today have a common

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.320
denominator, pilot.

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:04.520
Lately, pilots don't have to sit in the plane.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:07.680
ROV, or Remotely Operated Vehicles,

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.340
are flown by pilots outside of the plane

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:12.280
with the help of cutting-edge technology.

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.630
If we swing our attention over to NASA Dryden Flight Research

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.310
Center, you'll see what I'm talking about.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:20.220
Dryden is where some of the world's

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.340
most sophisticated and advanced aircraft

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:23.600
are operated and studied.

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:26.750
This is where some of the baddest aircraft ever flown

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:29.800
have been based, including the X-1 that Chuck Yeager used

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.760
the sound barrier, the X-15 which helped propel us into the space age, and the modified 747s that

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:41.480
carry the space shuttle. Tons of new aircraft are found here too. One of these new innovations with

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:48.000
wings is an Unmanned Aircraft System, or UAS, that is a redesigned Predator B. This plane has

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.340
been given the Native American name Econa, which in the Chukta language means intelligence.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:58.320
Wonder what NASA is doing with Econa in its fleet? Well, they definitely won't be flying any military

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.780
reconnaissance or attack missions.

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:01.010
So what else is there?

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:02.330
Well, research.

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:06.350
Econa was designed to fly for up to 30 hours at a time

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.390
and is able to reach altitudes above 12 kilometers,

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:10.660
or 40,000 feet.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:13.960
With a wingspan of 20 meters, or 66 feet,

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.340
the Econa is able to carry about 900 kilograms,

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:19.540
or one ton under its wings alone.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:21.820
This amount of space and strength

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:24.120
makes the versatile plane a perfect choice

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.300
for carrying all types of instruments and devices

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:27.700
for research and study.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:29.910
One of the instruments Econa

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.220
carries is the Autonomous Modular Scanner.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:35.010
This device operates like a digital camera.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.040
Unlike the cameras you're used to, however,

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:39.650
this one has specialized filters that

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:42.010
can detect infrared and thermal wavelengths.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.260
Although NASA uses this aircraft for all kinds

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:48.480
of basic research, the Econa is especially useful

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.840
to help firefighters control wildfires.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:54.100
That's right, during the annual wildfire season

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:55.840
out in the western part of the United States,

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:58.630
Econa can be seen flying high over the fires.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:02:59.760
It works like this.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.700
When a massive wildfire breaks out, it can be very hard to contain.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:09.020
So firefighters need up-to-the-minute information on where the hot spots are inside the fire.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.060
And they need to understand the progression of the fire over time.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.500
As you can imagine, it is pretty tough to get a clear image of these huge fires from the ground.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:20.640
And it can be even harder from the air due to all the smoke.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:22.860
This is where Econa comes in.

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:29.970
As Econa circles overhead, the autonomous modular sensor wildfire package can peer through thick smoke and haze.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.810
to record all the hotspots, the fire progression,

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:34.450
and changes in temperature.

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:37.370
These images are immediately processed

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:39.550
on board the aircraft, and then transmitted

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:41.990
through a communications satellite to NASA Ames

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:44.750
in Mountain View, California, where they are hosted

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:45.450
on a web server.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.640
These images can then be read and overlaid

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:51.780
onto Google Earth maps at the Interagency Fire Center

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:55.120
in Boise, Idaho, and local fire incident command centers

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:56.140
all over the country.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:58.750
Fire incident commanders on the ground

00:03:58.750 --> 00:03:59.970
can view these images

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.520
in three dimensions to help them route their firefighting resources where they need to go.

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:07.490
Sounds complicated and time-consuming.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:08.300
But…

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:09.650
It all happens in just minutes.

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:13.350
This technology is helping to contain and control fires.

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.340
saving millions of dollars in damage, and of course,

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:17.670
Saving lives, too.

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:21.220
And the Ikana is only one type of ROV.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:25.430
There are all sorts of unmanned aerial systems out there being studied at NASA,

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:29.810
including two global hawks, which will be used for Earth science starting in 2000.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.340
and several small unmanned aircraft.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:34.220
But that's another show.

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:38.540
Like in the past, as these aerial systems are studied and used for research,

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:42.440
the technology they use will eventually be the technology they improve.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:44.720
And the next time you see a plane overhead,

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:47.380
Remember what the first A in NASA stands for.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:49.750
You got it, Aeronautics.

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:53.230
Thanks for watching. I'm your host, Justin Tully.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:55.370
Catch you next time on NASA Launch Pad.
